# Resolution
Fixed in commit `1e8fde2d` (`Safely clean up conflicted stream appends`).

The fix replaces the unsafe `DeferredToSessionRecovery` no-op state with
reference-checked cleanup once command ownership becomes ambiguous. Before
deleting staged stream-append shards, cleanup now fences metadata-command
installation and scans the complete acting set for both durable segment
metadata and pending metadata commands that reference the exact shard keys.
Referenced payload is preserved, including when an idempotent competing append
has already published it or still owns it through a pending command. Payload
that has no exact reference is deleted. If the ownership scan cannot complete,
cleanup fails closed and retains the shards rather than risking deletion of
published data.

All ambiguous contention exits now invoke this reference-checked cleanup,
including unrelated pending-command failures, retry-budget exhaustion,
cleared-slot log collisions, and errors returned while draining a
`LogConflict`. The last case closes the remaining path where
`drain_after_object_pg_log_conflict` previously returned through `?` without
attempting cleanup.

Deterministic storage regressions cover both sides of the ownership decision:

- exact visible-pending and already-published competing appends retain their
  referenced payload and remain reconstructable;
- unrelated pending commands, unrelated install contention, duplicate
  segments, and a failed `LogConflict` drain remove unreferenced staged shards
  without publishing segment metadata.

Validation for the resolved implementation passed strict Clippy, focused
contention regressions, and the full repository suite: 7,037 tests passed.

# Summary
Introduced: the new deferred cleanup state suppresses deletion too early and too broadly. The previous implementation deleted the staged stream segment payload on errors before command ownership was established. The new implementation can skip cleanup even when no command owns the staged shard keys.
In commit 96e713b, stream append cleanup was changed from unconditional best-effort deletion on pre-commit errors to a state machine that skips cleanup once `payload_cleanup` is set to `DeferredToSessionRecovery`. The code sets this deferred state whenever any same-bucket pending metadata command is observed, and again before attempting to install the append command, without proving that the competing command is an idempotent append for the same session/segment or that it has adopted the same shard keys. After that point, several error paths either use `?` or invoke `cleanup_stream_append_payload!()`, but the macro becomes a no-op. If the pending command is unrelated, or if contention causes retry-budget exhaustion before this append is published, the payload shards written earlier are neither referenced by stream metadata nor deleted. A writer who can create same-bucket metadata contention can repeatedly upload large streaming segments and force append failures, leaving API-invisible shard files that can consume disk space.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm staged stream payload cleanup is gated by `StreamAppendPayloadCleanup::EagerAllowed` and becomes a no-op after deferral.
- [x] Confirm the code enters `DeferredToSessionRecovery` on any visible same-bucket pending command without checking that it owns or can publish the same shard keys.
- [x] Reproduce an error path after such unrelated pending-command drain, before the attempted staged payload is published to stream metadata.
- [x] Verify the error path returns without invoking shard deletion and without referencing the staged payload from stream metadata.
- [x] Verify the unreferenced staged shard files remain on disk/readable, demonstrating API-invisible storage consumption.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC and an LLDB trace. Code review confirmed the cleanup guard: `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:8980-8988` only deletes staged stream payload shards while `payload_cleanup == EagerAllowed`. The state is set to `DeferredToSessionRecovery` for any same-bucket pending command before proving relation to the current append at `cluster.rs:9017-9022`. Later, the duplicate segment error path calls `cleanup_stream_append_payload!()` at `cluster.rs:9044-9046`, but the macro is a no-op once deferred.

I added `validation_unrelated_pending_duplicate_segment_leaks_staged_payload` in `crates/storage/src/cluster/local/tests/stream_commands.rs:4937-5124`. The test commits a legitimate stream segment, writes a second conflicting staged payload for the same segment index, injects an unrelated same-bucket pending `CreateStreamUpload` command (`stream_commands.rs:5034-5064`), then calls the real `commit_stream_segment_append` path (`stream_commands.rs:5075-5084`). The command fails with `InvalidRequest { reason: "duplicate segment_index 0" }`, but the cleanup hook count remains zero and all staged shard files remain on disk (`stream_commands.rs:5085-5106`). Metadata still lists only the original committed segment, while direct shard reads using the leaked shard keys/acks succeed (`stream_commands.rs:5112-5123`).

Reproduction command output: `cargo test -p storage validation_unrelated_pending_duplicate_segment_leaks_staged_payload -- --nocapture` produced: `validation_error=InvalidRequest { reason: "duplicate segment_index 0" }`, `cleanup_attempts=0`, and three `leaked_shard_exists=true` shard paths, then the test passed. A normal crash attempt was not applicable because this is a logical cleanup/leak bug, not memory corruption. Valgrind was not available in the container. GDB was not installed, but LLDB was available. LLDB breakpoint at `cluster.rs:9046` showed execution on the duplicate error path immediately after the cleanup macro call, with `frame variable payload_cleanup` reporting `DeferredToSessionRecovery`, proving the deletion call was suppressed.

